tree-sitter-python: serialize an indent only when both of its bytes fit

serialize writes two bytes of state, one byte for each open string
delimiter, and then two bytes for each indentation level, for as long as
fewer than 1024 bytes are written. With an odd number of bytes ahead of
the indents, the last one can start at byte 1023 and end at byte 1025,
past the 1024 bytes tree-sitter gives a scanner's state: a string inside
511 nested blocks does it. With its assertions on, tree-sitter's parser
aborts the process on the length the scanner returns; without them, the
byte past the buffer is overwritten.

An indent is written only when both of its bytes fit; deeper levels are
left out, and the parse of their lines degrades to an error.

Applies to src/scanner.c of tree-sitter-python at commit
26855eabccb19c6abf499fbc5b8dc7cc9ab8bc64.

diff --git a/src/scanner.c b/src/scanner.c
index 1fc77cdbdee6c7d33c183dc8c2bc3b64c9b0a410..f50fc5d296239499db36a70895f90f6af6fef07e 100644
--- a/src/scanner.c
+++ b/src/scanner.c
@@ -380,7 +380,9 @@ unsigned tree_sitter_python_external_scanner_serialize(void *payload, char *buff
     size += delimiter_count;
 
     uint32_t iter = 1;
-    for (; iter < scanner->indents.size && size < TREE_SITTER_SERIALIZATION_BUFFER_SIZE; ++iter) {
+    // Each indent takes two bytes; one that would end past the buffer is
+    // left out, with every indent after it.
+    for (; iter < scanner->indents.size && size + 2 <= TREE_SITTER_SERIALIZATION_BUFFER_SIZE; ++iter) {
         uint16_t indent_value = *array_get(&scanner->indents, iter);
         buffer[size++] = (char)(indent_value & 0xFF);
         buffer[size++] = (char)((indent_value >> 8) & 0xFF);
